  "query_preview": "..\nMonday through Tuesday: This week will be off to a warm start as \nridging over the region promotes an increase in temperatures. Today, \nhighs are in the mid 80s to low 90s, just a few degrees above \nnormal. The ridge will continue strengthening, bringing a nearly 10 \ndegree increase in temperatures between today and tomorrow, bringing \nhighs into the mid 90s to low triple digits. This will lead to a \nreturn of widespread Moderate HeatRisk with very isolated values of \nMajor HeatRisk. People, especially those sensitive to heat related \nillnesses, should take precautions to stay adequately cool and \nhydrated. Precipitation chances through Tuesday are near zero. \n\nWednesday: A low pressure off the PNW coast will begin nudging into \nthe state of Washington, decreasing temperatures and bringing slight \nchances of precipitation to the mountains. Highs on Wednesday will \nbe in the low to mid 90s, with widespread Moderate HeatRisk still \nexpected. An increase in PWATs from moisture being advected into the \narea combined with daytime heating will result in a 10 percent \nchance of isolated thunderstorms in mountain areas, specifically in \nthe Idaho Panhandle and northern mountains. Current soundings show \nPWATs in the 0.8-0.9 range and DCAPE values of 1000+ J/kg, \nindicating that biggest threats with these storms will be heavy \ndownpours and gusty outflow winds near 40 mph. Winds will increase \nthrough the afternoon and evening with the approaching low, which \nwhen combined with warm temperatures and low RHs across the area \ncould result in fire weather concerns.\n\nThursday through Sunday: The low will then move through the state of \nWashington and Idaho Thursday, then 100 percent of clusters have the \nlow remaining over the PNW through the weekend as a broad upper \nlevel ridge over the central US keeps it in place. This will\nresult in chances for mountain precipitation and breezy to \ngusty winds through much of the area on Thursday. Additionally, \ntemperatures will drop from the mid 90s to the mid 80s as the \nlow advects in cooler air, continuing to cool over the weekend. \nChances for precipitation are less certain due to the strength \nand placement of the low, and continue to be low and confined to\nthe mountains. /AS",
